WEBVTT

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:16.550
Akagi Shigeru won two million 
from the bet against Fake Akagi.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:20.500
On top of that, he took his 
coworkers for all they were worth

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:24.420
by turning Kawashima's own 
cheat against them.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.000
But this was not well-received...

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.540
You screwed us.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.970
You're going to give us our money back.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.810
No, just returning it won't do.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:38.510
You'll have to compensate 
us for what you did.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.950
That's right, Akagi.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.510
I think that two million from before will do.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:51.520
Looks like you understand.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:04.400
I'll spare you.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.900
But you'll have to clean up this mess.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:20.620
Akagi-san!

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.360
Please take me with you!

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.480
I want to become like you!

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.110
If you hang around me,

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:31.080
you'll be dragged into 
whatever messes I get into.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:37.190
Who are these people?

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:38.500
Akagi.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:45.440
Our boss insists on seeing a match 
between you and Fake Akagi.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:51.090
<b>Episode 10</b>

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:51.090
<b>The Announcement of the Counterattack</b>

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.380
He's playing against the rep 
player of the Fujisawa Group.

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:57.200
He'll play you after that's done.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:07.460
Akagi.

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:10.410
Just wait, it'll be done soon.

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:11.930
How's it going?

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:15.370
They agreed to play 4 East-South matches.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:17.060
He won the first and second.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:19.240
Right now, it's the last hand of the third.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:23.290
He's got a 12,000 lead on the 
Fujisawa Group's rep player, Urabe.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:25.180
It's very likely he'll win again.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.330
That's about right.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:30.920
I'll crush this loser within an hour.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.920
And then, you'll be next, Akagi.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:40.020
The 14th turn.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:43.190
Ron!

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:48.810
It's just a tanyao hand, 
but I stay dealer.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:50.660
Not bad, not bad.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:52.050
This was just the beginning.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:55.070
Sorry, Urabe-san.

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:00.680
Head bump - When two players call ron, the player closest 
to the discarder (in turn order) gets priority.

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:00.680
Fake Akagi does a head bump.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:03.150
I don't believe it!

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:07.380
<i>He's won three out of four. 
I guess it's over.</i>

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:12.890
Damn it!

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:15.570
Ishikawa-san, let's go.

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:17.130
You have a waiting room, right?

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:18.830
What?

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:20.860
Yeah, it's this way.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:24.100
<i>What's with that kid?</i>

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:29.840
Akagi-san, that guy with the 
sunglasses is really good.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.670
You're going to play against him next, right?

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:34.000
Who knows?

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.000
"Who knows"?

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:36.850
I thought that's why you're here.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:42.300
He might get crushed 
before he can play me.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:46.040
It looks like that Urabe guy 
is one or two levels better.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:46.780
What?

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:47.820
But...

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:52.010
Oh, Akagi, I'm glad you made it.

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:53.280
I've been looking forward

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.420
to today's main attraction, the match 
between the old and new Akagi—

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:57.030
Boss...

00:04:57.560 --> 00:04:58.460
What is it?

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:01.080
Well... Akagi's saying that our rep player

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:04.450
might be crushed by Urabe 
before we get to that.

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:06.040
What do you mean, Akagi?

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:12.550
Urabe is just baiting Fake Akagi.

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:15.220
He's losing on purpose.

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:19.420
When Fake Akagi scored his 
third consecutive win,

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.160
this was Urabe's discard pile.

00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:25.260
And this was his hand.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:27.900
When I walked in,

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:31.560
Urabe was just discarding the 2 of 
characters that he had just drawn.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:34.070
After that, he immediately 
discarded every tile he drew.

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:36.690
There's something strange about that.

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:40.520
The 5 of bamboo he discarded 
in the 10th turn was a dora.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:43.510
It's strange because, after that turn, 
he discarded all tiles as he drew them.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:47.200
It's easy to see if you consider how 
his hand looked in the 10th turn.

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:51.520
Osamu, what would you 
discard from this hand?

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:52.090
Huh?

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:53.520
Hmm...

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:55.780
The 5 of bamboo is a dora.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:57.660
So I'd try to go for a sanshoku...

00:05:58.130 --> 00:06:00.310
and depending on my opponents' discards,

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:02.080
I'd discard the 8 of bamboo 
or 4 of characters.

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:08.500
His direct opponent, Fake Akagi, 
had these in his discards.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:10.530
Then I'd discard the 4 of characters.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:12.130
If I wanted to play it safe,

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:13.670
the 7 of characters 
would be an option, too.

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:15.390
That's right.

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:20.110
Usually you'd want to go for a solid 
tanyao, sanshoku, one dora hand.

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:23.880
Seeing that he discarded 
the 5 of bamboo

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:26.320
and didn't declare riichi 
 can only mean one thing:

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:29.050
He didn't want to win.

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:34.450
If he got any reverse dora, 
it might have caused an upset.

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:38.330
By playing like this, he put 
Fake Akagi in first place

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:42.830
and lost big, but he'll jack up the 
wager and get it back next time.

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:44.070
Or...

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:45.070
Or?

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:51.080
He'll pull something in the 
final East-South match.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:53.890
Boss!

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:56.310
Urabe says he has something 
serious to discuss.

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:59.080
What is it?

00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:03.820
Well, I don't know what to say.

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:07.190
I just can't face my boss like this.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:12.220
So would it be possible to double the original 
wager of 500,000 per East-South match

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:17.240
to one million, just for the final match?

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:18.540
One million?

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:24.800
Yes, but since we're already 
playing for this huge sum,

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:29.810
I think it'd be a shame if it ended 
with us both in second and third place.

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:33.720
So how about we keep playing until 
one of us is in first place?

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:37.820
So if neither of us is in first place, 
we replay the whole East-South match?

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:39.240
Right.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:41.650
I knew you were quick!

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:46.580
And let's say, if that happens, the same 
conditions will apply for the next match.

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:48.190
Overtime, if you will.

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:49.630
Piss off!

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:53.180
I don't have time to play 
silly games with you.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:54.010
I've got to—

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:55.640
Calm down.

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:58.690
It's nothing to raise your voice over.

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:01.100
Accept it.

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:03.980
I'll have the other man 
wait as long as necessary.

00:08:04.310 --> 00:08:08.970
You just have to finish the next 
East-South match in first place.

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:10.650
It's simple.

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:13.510
If you say so, Boss.

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:16.760
Are you sure?

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.100
It's exactly as Akagi predicted.

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:23.880
I want to see it with my own eyes...

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:25.850
The skill Urabe has that Akagi claims

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:29.840
puts him one or two levels 
above our rep player.

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:32.840
With the wager doubled,

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:37.210
one million is now riding on the 
fourth and final East-South match.

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:39.690
Everybody who heard Akagi's prediction

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:43.630
assumed that Urabe's 
counterattack would begin now.

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:45.220
However...

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:46.050
Ron!

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:48.560
Tanyao, two dora. 5,200.

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:52.640
Urabe is not doing well.

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:55.330
Fake Akagi, meanwhile, is in great shape.

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:57.030
He secured his first 
place from the beginning

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:58.380
and is setting the pace for the game.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:01.270
The last hand.

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:03.500
Fake Akagi is in first place.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:05.510
Yoshikawa is in second place.

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:07.870
Finally, Urabe is in third place.

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:11.430
It seems that the match will end before
Urabe gets his chance to shine,

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:13.730
but he does make a move in the final hand.

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:15.250
Pon.

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:18.480
<i>Obviously a single suit hand.</i>

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:20.050
<i>Chinitsu, toitoi.</i>

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:22.420
<i>Throw in a tanyao and it'll be a baiman.</i>

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:25.060
<i>It's a hand that could 
put him into first place.</i>

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:27.990
Fake Akagi is in tenpai.

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:31.230
<i>I won't deal into your obvious chinitsu hand.</i>

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:34.570
<i>Yoshikawa's riichi is a greater danger, 
since he's in second place.</i>

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:37.950
<i>With his riichi wager out, 
we're 4,700 apart.</i>

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:40.040
<i>If I'm not careful, he'll overtake me.</i>

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:42.020
<i>I wouldn't want that.</i>

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:45.540
<i>So I should discard a 
4 of bamboo, a safe tile.</i>

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:50.420
Sorry, bro, that's it.

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:51.630
Ron!

00:09:53.870 --> 00:09:56.320
Tanyao, toitoi. 5,200.

00:09:57.980 --> 00:09:59.970
<i>A single wait for the 4 of bamboo?!</i>

00:09:59.970 --> 00:10:02.430
<i>If he had gone for a single 
wait for the 2 of bamboo,</i>

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:04.330
<i>he could have had a baiman!</i>

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:06.180
Screw this!

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:09.930
You won't ever be able to get 
first place if you play like this!

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:11.950
You aim too low!

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:15.480
It's fine.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:17.940
Surviving is what's important.

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:20.180
Besides, by going into the next match,

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:24.280
the wager will grow from 
one to two million.

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:27.190
Grow from one to two million?

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:28.600
Yes.

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:30.030
We said that the wager

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:33.020
would stay the same and we'd 
go into the next match!

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:35.690
That's not what I said.

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:39.270
I said that the same conditions 
apply for the next match.

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:42.980
The conditions included doubling the 
wager from 500,000 to one million.

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:45.540
That's the condition I'm talking about.

00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:49.570
If I wanted to stay at one million,

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:53.100
I would have said that we'll 
keep the stakes the same.

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:55.570
I wouldn't have gone out of my way to 
say that the same conditions apply.

00:10:57.030 --> 00:10:59.650
Screw you! I won't accept that.

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:03.730
Cut the shit, you con artist!

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:11.560
I don't care if you accept or not.

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:16.150
What matters is what the one 
with the actual authority says.

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:18.630
Boss, what's it gonna be?

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:20.140
You'll accept, right?

00:11:21.710 --> 00:11:24.610
I believe my request is reasonable.

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:28.750
I wouldn't even dare to 
dream that the boss

00:11:28.750 --> 00:11:35.320
of the great Kawada Group 
would crush such clear logic.

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:36.880
Will you accept?

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:40.890
Boss, please! I beg you.

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:43.990
All right, I accept.

00:11:43.990 --> 00:11:46.500
Boss, you don't have to accept.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:48.580
That's playing into his hand!

00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:51.030
But he does have a point.

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:52.000
Boss...

00:11:54.010 --> 00:11:57.500
All you need to do is win the next match.

00:11:58.090 --> 00:11:59.410
No need to think too much.

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:05.600
The game resumes with the new rules.

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:09.880
The game will continue until Urabe 
or Fake Akagi finish in first place.

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:12.600
If both players are in 
second place or lower,

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:16.020
the wager is doubled and they'll 
play another East-South match.

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:19.960
Of course, Fake Akagi wouldn't want that.

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:22.560
Even though he tries his best to 
get first place, it's fruitless.

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:25.930
This is not surprising because 
Urabe isn't even concerned

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:27.850
with getting first place himself.

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:30.570
He's focusing on boosting someone else.

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:33.940
Ron.

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:37.940
In Mahjong, it's a lot simpler 
to prevent someone from

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:40.580
getting into first place than to 
get into first place yourself.

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:45.220
All you have to do is give your 
own points to another player.

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:48.400
The game carries over.

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:52.960
The wager grows from two million 
to four million, then to eight million.

00:12:54.760 --> 00:12:56.220
<i>I'm going for first place,</i>

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:59.330
<i>but all he's doing is preventing 
me from getting there.</i>

00:12:59.870 --> 00:13:01.780
<i>Just because it's not 
working out the way I want</i>

00:13:01.780 --> 00:13:04.780
<i>doesn't mean I'm a worse player than he is.</i>

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:09.530
Another draw.

00:13:09.530 --> 00:13:11.380
The wager has reached 16 million.

00:13:11.950 --> 00:13:19.390
Today, the 16 million yen of 1964 
would be worth over 160 million yen.

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:23.250
<b>Fujisawa Group Boss
Fujisawa</b>

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:26.060
Boss Fujisawa.

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:28.970
It's been a while, Kawada-san.

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:35.070
Well, I received a call... 
that the wager was skyrocketing.

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:38.070
I'm going to be a witness.

00:13:43.010 --> 00:13:46.080
To protect Urabe and these new rules.

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:47.750
Protect?

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:51.990
Yes. Humans are nothing more than scum.

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:54.020
As the wager gets higher,

00:13:54.020 --> 00:13:57.500
their true nature comes out 
and they lose their dignity.

00:13:57.500 --> 00:14:00.390
Going back on their word becomes natural.

00:14:00.860 --> 00:14:02.980
And if someone complains about that,

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:06.350
who knows to what levels they will stoop.

00:14:06.350 --> 00:14:10.500
Boss Fujisawa, don't you think 
that's a bit out of line?

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:11.440
No, no!

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:16.140
I'm certain that Kawada-san would 
never do something like that.

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:22.650
But I just got worried because 
the wager will continue to grow.

00:14:25.450 --> 00:14:27.100
The game resumes.

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:29.380
The wager is at 16 million.

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:32.390
<i>I'm going to put my aspirations 
for first place on hold for now.</i>

00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:34.300
<i>I'll leave him in the dust</i>

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:37.130
<i>in the second or the second to 
last hand with a quick sprint!</i>

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:41.130
<i>I'll take advantage of the weakness 
in his plan, this wager doubling!</i>

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:43.680
And in the second to last hand...

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:45.870
Fake Akagi is in tenpai 
during the eighth turn.

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:47.900
<i>There it is.</i>

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:49.500
<i>The ideal 6 of characters.</i>

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:52.320
With this hand, 
usually the 2 of circles

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:54.730
would be discarded for 
the 6 of characters.

00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:57.520
It's standard to wait for the
1, 4, and 7 of characters.

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:00.250
<i>But here...</i>

00:15:02.020 --> 00:15:04.220
<i>I'm going to go for a single 2 of circles wait!</i>

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:05.880
<i>The player after me, Ota,</i>

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:08.460
<i>most likely has a circles honitsu.</i>

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:11.680
<i>The tiles around 2 and 5 are 
obviously in his sights.</i>

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:12.960
<i>There's no way I could discard that.</i>

00:15:13.310 --> 00:15:16.930
<i>So if I go for a 2 of characters wait, 
no one will deal into it.</i>

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:20.070
<i>Usually, that would mean 
I'd have to draw it myself,</i>

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:23.920
<i>but there's one person who 
will discard it freely.</i>

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:25.340
<i>Ota is in first place,</i>

00:15:25.340 --> 00:15:28.060
<i>so Urabe wants to give him 
another push to end this match.</i>

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:30.410
<i>It's very probable that he 
will deal into his hand.</i>

00:15:30.760 --> 00:15:33.780
<i>And I'll snipe that 2 of characters 
when he discards it.</i>

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:36.400
Ron!

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:39.850
Sorry, head bump.

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:42.090
Red dragon, three dora. Mangan.

00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:45.710
Boss.

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:50.870
<i>Now, if I just win 1,000 points in 
the next hand, I'll be in first place.</i>

00:15:50.870 --> 00:15:54.330
<i>Then you'll be the one who'll 
have to put up the 16 million.</i>

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:56.630
<i>I've got you cornered, Urabe!</i>

00:15:58.710 --> 00:16:00.840
That was so close.

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:08.850
If you had gotten that win with the 
last hand, you could have ended it.

00:16:08.850 --> 00:16:12.940
You can't win unless you win 
one more time in the last hand.

00:16:13.390 --> 00:16:16.350
That just goes to show 
that your luck is lacking.

00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:18.800
You're the one without luck!

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:20.690
I only need to win 1,000 points and—

00:16:22.090 --> 00:16:26.410
And I'm telling you that the path to 
those 1,000 points will be very thorny.

00:16:26.410 --> 00:16:28.700
With those sissy feet of yours...

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:31.570
You won't make it through!

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:37.910
<i>Thorny path, my ass!</i>

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:41.150
<i>All I need to do is win before anyone else.</i>

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:45.150
<i>Come on, a fast hand!</i>

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:47.450
<i>I got it!</i>

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:49.980
<i>The ideal starting hand.</i>

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:51.960
<i>This is the luck I was born with.</i>

00:16:52.310 --> 00:16:54.590
<i>I'm in a different league than Urabe.</i>

00:16:55.530 --> 00:16:57.190
And in the ninth turn...

00:16:57.960 --> 00:16:58.960
Chi!

00:17:00.590 --> 00:17:01.950
Fake Akagi is in tenpai.

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:03.530
A closed wait for the 6 of bamboo.

00:17:03.530 --> 00:17:04.970
<i>I got you.</i>

00:17:05.320 --> 00:17:07.010
<i>Thorny path, my ass.</i>

00:17:07.010 --> 00:17:08.800
<i>You'll be the laughingstock.</i>

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:09.970
Riichi!

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:15.450
<i>I thought his plan was 
to deal into Ota's hand.</i>

00:17:15.850 --> 00:17:17.570
<i>Did he think Ota was taking too long</i>

00:17:17.570 --> 00:17:21.150
<i>and figure that he has to take 
matters into his own hands?</i>

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:25.050
But in the next turn...

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:26.690
Kan.

00:17:29.730 --> 00:17:31.780
<i>Then why did he declare a riichi?</i>

00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:34.030
<i>The 1 han from the green 
dragons would be enough...</i>

00:17:34.540 --> 00:17:38.230
<i>I get it. Right now we're 
17,500 points apart.</i>

00:17:38.820 --> 00:17:40.900
<i>Riichi, green dragon, sanshoku...</i>

00:17:40.900 --> 00:17:43.640
<i>He only needs 4 han...</i>

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:46.060
<i>He's thinking he'll take the lead if he can</i>

00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:47.800
<i>get two reverse dora on top.</i>

00:17:48.410 --> 00:17:49.640
<i>Damn it!</i>

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:54.680
He got the 8 of bamboo 
while waiting for the 6.

00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:57.150
Usually this would be the time 
to switch to a double-sided

00:17:57.150 --> 00:17:59.150
wait by discarding a 5 of bamboo, but...

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:03.530
<i>The 5 of bamboo is risky.</i>

00:18:04.090 --> 00:18:05.360
<i>I've got no choice.</i>

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:11.770
Fake Akagi decides to stick with 
the closed 6 of bamboo wait.

00:18:12.430 --> 00:18:14.500
However, Urabe's next turn...

00:18:15.690 --> 00:18:17.340
The 5 of bamboo would have been safe.

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:22.050
Ultimately, this mistake will prove fatal.

00:18:26.260 --> 00:18:27.580
His decision completely backfires.

00:18:29.830 --> 00:18:31.400
<i>I can't believe it.</i>

00:18:31.400 --> 00:18:34.180
<i>If I had been a bit more bold, 
I would have had this.</i>

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:35.710
<i>This sucks.</i>

00:18:36.570 --> 00:18:38.190
<i>But I won't get desperate.</i>

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:40.880
<i>I would like to get rid 
of this 9 right away,</i>

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:42.210
<i>but it's risky with Urabe.</i>

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:44.800
<i>I shouldn't overextend myself here.</i>

00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:47.470
Fake Akagi throws away 
his 6 of bamboo wait.

00:18:48.710 --> 00:18:49.970
However, in the next turn...

00:18:51.160 --> 00:18:52.210
That backfires yet again.

00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:01.490
Fake Akagi waits for a 2, 5, or 8 of bamboo, 
as he can no longer with with a 6.

00:19:01.990 --> 00:19:04.350
<i>What is he doing?</i>

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:05.490
<i>It doesn't make any sense!</i>

00:19:05.490 --> 00:19:08.000
<i>If you're going to discard 
the 9 of bamboo here,</i>

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:09.540
<i>why didn't you do it before?</i>

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:10.730
<i>If you had done it earlier,</i>

00:19:10.730 --> 00:19:12.460
<i>it would have been 
a step towards victory,</i>

00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:15.590
<i>but doing it now is just for 
maintaining your tenpai.</i>

00:19:16.270 --> 00:19:19.480
<i>He's no different than a 
confused amateur right now.</i>

00:19:20.480 --> 00:19:24.100
Fake Akagi draws a risky tile this time.

00:19:24.100 --> 00:19:27.430
His hand was never backed 
by determination.

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:29.610
He takes apart his tenpai.

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:34.140
After that, there's no stopping it.

00:19:34.850 --> 00:19:37.620
In a few turns, his hand 
is a complete mess,

00:19:37.620 --> 00:19:39.850
as if it was reflecting his state of mind.

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:41.710
He falls apart.

00:19:45.090 --> 00:19:45.910
Tsumo!

00:19:47.660 --> 00:19:49.800
Just before the hand ends in a draw,

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:53.430
Ota rises to the top with 
his draw, causing an upset.

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:56.100
Fake Akagi missed his chance to win.

00:19:56.100 --> 00:19:57.500
But...

00:19:57.500 --> 00:20:03.710
Despite all that, Fake Akagi is relieved 
that Urabe's haneman did not materialize.

00:20:04.130 --> 00:20:05.860
<i>He's lacking something.</i>

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:10.210
<i>He's lacking something crucial 
that every gambler needs.</i>

00:20:11.370 --> 00:20:15.720
<i>Urabe and this kid really 
are in different classes.</i>

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:19.410
At this time, Urabe's hand 
is actually a complete mess.

00:20:19.410 --> 00:20:22.430
The closed kan he made after 
the riichi was just bluff.

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:25.320
Forceful play without any safety net.

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:26.940
A Mahjong game of brute strength.

00:20:27.440 --> 00:20:29.880
Compared to that, 
Fake Akagi is weak.

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:34.130
Victory is something you 
attain with equal risk.

00:20:34.130 --> 00:20:35.940
He avoided that.

00:20:37.050 --> 00:20:38.210
Ishikawa.

00:20:38.210 --> 00:20:38.940
Yes?

00:20:39.290 --> 00:20:42.210
Can we call in the real Akagi?

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:44.960
This man is no good.

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:46.550
There's something he lacks.

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:53.160
Indeed, Fake Akagi was missing the most 
crucial quality that gamblers must have.

00:20:54.260 --> 00:20:56.720
Akagi's prediction became reality.

00:20:57.260 --> 00:21:00.000
Urabe continues to push Fake Akagi 
further into the corner.

00:21:01.600 --> 00:21:04.450
The legendary man, Akagi Shigeru...

00:21:05.300 --> 00:21:08.530
quietly waits for his time.

00:22:15.970 --> 00:22:19.360
Next time, 
"The Foundation for Despair."

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>Next Episode</b>

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>The Foundation for Despair</b>
